[
{"id": "question 1449", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "The figure 0.33 dB cited in 3GPP TR 38.884 §7.1 is explicitly the relative increase from 43.5 GHz to 48.2 GHz (band n259 -> n262 extension), not to 71 GHz. For 43.5 GHz to 71 GHz (FR2-2 n263), TR 38.884 §7.2.3 uses 10.3 dB cable loss at 1 m at 71 GHz while the 43.5 GHz base is roughly 6.8 dB/m, yielding a relative increase of roughly 3.5 dB/m (option 3).", "source": "3GPP TR 38.884 v18.0.0 §7.1 and §7.2.3 (TSGS#100-e RP-2007-08)"},
{"id": "question 1456", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "3GPP TS 38.331/215 §5.8.5/5.8.6 and TR 37.985 §5.1.2.1 define four SL synchronization sources for NR SL: GNSS, gNB/eNB, SyncRef UE, and the UE's internal clock. None of the four options lists all four sources; the marked option only lists GNSS and gNB/eNB (the two highest-quality sources), which is incomplete.", "source": "3GPP TR 37.985 v19.0.0 §5.1.2.1; 3GPP TS 38.331 §5.8"},
{"id": "question 1459", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "3GPP TS 22.245/23.002/TS 22.101 don't have a direct clause on 'mapping between service data of different domains' with the wording shown. The closest 3GPP statement is that the visited network handles certain mappings for service-based roaming, and the home environment manages subscriber services generally. The marked answer (home environment, option 2) is plausible but the 3GPP wording is not crisp enough to call it uniquely right.", "source": "own knowledge - no direct primary source found"},
{"id": "question 1463", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "TS 32.291 v18.12.0 §5.1 Table 5.1-1 explicitly states: 'The \"Nchf_SpendingLimitControl\" service is defined in 29.594'. None of the listed options (TS 23.203, TS 32.27x, TS 23.502, TS 23.272) is TS 29.594, so no offered option is correct.", "source": "ETSI TS 132 291 V18.12.0 §5.1 (Table 5.1-1)"},
{"id": "question 1476", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "3GPP TS 22.261 §7.6.1 explicitly states: 'To support VR environments with low motion-to-photon capabilities, the 5G system shall support: motion-to-photon latency in the range of 7 ms to 15 ms...'.", "source": "3GPP TS 22.261 v19.x §7.6.1 (also reproduced in TS 38.824 Annex A.1.4)"},
{"id": "question 1480", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "3GPP-explorer glossary for HR-SBO (Release 18): 'In HR-SBO, the Protocol Data Unit (PDU) Session is still anchored at the Home UPF... However, the architecture introduces a key enhancement: the ability to create a local breakout path for specific, authorized traffic flows directly within the Visited PLMN (VPLMN).' Matches 'to enable local traffic routing in the visited PLMN'.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.548 (Rel-18 HR-SBO additions); 3GPP TS 29.512 v18 §HR-SBO"},
{"id": "question 1495", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "3GPP TS 38.300 §7.5: 'The UE reports its UE radio access capabilities which are static at least when the network requests. The gNB can request what capabilities for the UE to report based on band information.' This directly matches option 1.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.300 §7.5"},
{"id": "question 1508", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "3GPP TS 36.304 §5.3.2: 'For UE camping on E-UTRA connected to 5GC, the UE shall ignore Access Category and Identity related cell access restrictions for cell reselection. A change of the indicated access restriction shall not trigger cell reselection by the UE.'", "source": "3GPP TS 36.304 v15.8.0+ §5.3.2"},
{"id": "question 1512", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "3GPP TS 28.104 §7.2.1.3 Paging optimization analysis description: 'This repetitive paging attempts result in the wastage of network resources... It is desirable to use MDAS (Management data analytic service) to optimize the current paging procedures... thereby ensuring the efficient paging procedures and optimal network resource utilization, as paging can be initiated only when there are more chances for it to be successful.' Matches 'Avoiding wastage of network resources'.", "source": "3GPP TS 28.104 v17.x §7.2.1.3"},
{"id": "question 1538", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "3GPP TS 38.101-2 (and -4/-5) repeatedly defines it as: 'OTA sensitivity is defined as the minimum mean power received at the RIB for a specified reference measurement channel' (i.e. the EIS at which a reference FRC achieves a target throughput). Options 4 and 5 describe selectivity/co-channel tolerance, not sensitivity. Marked option 5 is a misdefinition.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.101-2 §10.3.2; TS 38.101-5 §10.3.2; FR2-1 SNR derivation"},
{"id": "question 1545", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "3GPP TR 22.847 §4.1 'Multi-modal service' states: 'With the benefit of combining input from more than one source and/or output to more than one destination...'. TeleQnA standard's specification excerpt also matches: 'a service that supports applications enabling input from more than one sources and/or output to more than one destinations'.", "source": "3GPP TR 22.847 (Rel-17/18) §4.1"},
{"id": "question 1550", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "3GPP TR 37.817 (Rel-17 study on AI/ML for NG-RAN energy saving) lists 'Inaccurate cell load prediction' among the explicit challenges of RAN energy saving schemes; load-prediction errors cause ping-pong switch-on/off and neighbour overload. Marked option is a defensible cited challenge. (Note: option 5 'Conflicting targets...' is also listed, so the question is arguably multi-defensible; the marked answer is still defensible.)", "source": "3GPP TR 37.817 v17.0.0 §5.x energy-saving challenges"},
{"id": "question 1583", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "3GPP WI database: 'OAM14-FILMEAS – Filtering of PM measurements and data volume' (item 710009). It affects specs 32.130, 32.412, 32.416, 32.425 (all Performance Management / fault & performance measurement specs), not cross-operator accounting. Option 5 'Use cases and requirements for management of fault and performance measurements' matches 'PM measurements'. The marked option (cross-operator accounting) is unrelated.", "source": "3GPP Work Item 710009 OAM14-FILMEAS; WhatTheSpeeds / 3gpp.org WiCr--710009.htm"},
{"id": "question 1652", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "IEEE 802.11-2016/802.11ad DMG clause: a DMG protected period is a time period during which other DMG STAs are restricted from transmitting so that the SP of an SP-owner STA is protected from interference. The purpose is to minimize interference through transmission restrictions during a specific period (option 1). Option 2 also captures the same idea more verbosely; option 1 is the closer standard-form paraphrase.", "source": "IEEE Std 802.11-2016 (DMG clause); 802.11ad/ay scheduling literature"},
{"id": "question 1690", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "3GPP TS 29.525 §6.4.2.15 (AfGuideURSP): 'DNN (within the \"dnn\" attribute of the RouteSelectionParameterSet data type) and S-NSSAI (within the \"snssai\" attribute...) from the route selection parameter set are used to set the Route selection descriptor contents'. Matches option 1.", "source": "3GPP TS 29.525 v18.5.0 §6.4.2.15"},
{"id": "question 1700", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "3GPP TR 21.905 vocabulary: 'Best effort QoS: The lowest of all QoS traffic classes.' Verbatim match with the marked option 1.", "source": "3GPP TR 21.905 (Vocabulary for 3GPP Specifications)"},
{"id": "question 1707", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "3GPP TS 38.300 §10.1 'Basic Scheduler Operation': 'Taking into account the UE buffer status and the QoS requirements of each UE and associated radio bearers, schedulers assign resources between UEs'. The QoS-requirements aspect (option 1) is the primary, mandatory criterion.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.300 v18 §10.1"},
{"id": "question 1719", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "IEEE 802.15.4e default hopping sequence: the SHUFFLE array used to build the default FHS is 'equivalent to the outputs of a 9-bit linear feedback shift register (LFSR) with polynomial x^9 + x^5 + 1'. So the default hopping sequence is generated via an LFSR. Marked option 2 is correct.", "source": "IEEE Std 802.15.4-2015/4e (TSCH default hopping sequence); US patent US9614579B2"}
]